# internal/platform/database/

The PostgreSQL connection pool and the migration runner live here: pool
construction and configuration, the readiness check that pings it, and the
Goose runner that applies `db/migrations/`.

`OpenMigrator` builds an instance-scoped Goose provider over the migrations
filesystem supplied by `db.Migrations`. It uses a dedicated one-connection
`database/sql` pool and a PostgreSQL session advisory lock. It does not use
Goose's legacy package-global filesystem or migration registry, and opening
it never applies schema changes.

`CheckCompatibility` is a separate read-only path used by startup and
`db status`. It accepts empty or valid-but-behind history, and fails closed
for ahead versions, malformed/partial history, or a nonempty current schema
without Goose history. It never creates `goose_db_version`; only an explicit
`db migrate` action mutates schema.

`pool.go` applies explicit conservative limits instead of inheriting
pgx's CPU-dependent maximum. `internal/app/app.go` wires the pool into
three seams:

- `health.Prober` runs the pool ping for readiness.
- `runtime/observability`'s `PoolMetrics` accepts a `PoolStatsFunc`. The
  pool reports live counters through it.
- `NewTransactor` adapts `pgxpool.BeginTx` to the bounded
  `runtime/transaction` lifecycle. Use-case callbacks receive the concrete
  `pgx.Tx` and pass it to their feature-local sqlc `New` constructor.

Repositories receive this pool, or a transaction implementing the same
sqlc-generated DBTX interface. They do not construct pools and do not begin
transactions. Nested `Within` calls made with the callback context fail
instead of creating a hidden savepoint or independent inner commit.

`dbtest.New(t)` creates a unique real database per integration test from the
administrative `TEST_DATABASE_URL`, enforces an eight-connection server limit,
and force-drops it during cleanup. Missing configuration skips locally and
fails under `CI=true` or `CI=1`; the test role must have `CREATEDB` only on a
disposable test server.

`migration_matrix_test.go` proves both a clean install and every supported
release upgrade. Historical SQL snapshots live under
`testdata/migration-upgrades/`, outside the production migration embed. Never
edit a fixture after releasing it; add a new versioned snapshot and keep older
fixtures for as long as that direct upgrade path is supported. Run the matrix
with `make migration-test`.

`sqlcsafety` is the stdlib-only companion to `sqlc vet`. Its project test
walks feature-local configs, requires generated Go output to remain exactly in
that feature's `store/`, and requires the canonical unbounded-DELETE,
unbounded-UPDATE, and no-TRUNCATE CEL rules unchanged and enabled. sqlc itself
remains the YAML and SQL parser; run both layers with `make sqlc-vet`.

`directpgx_test.go` is a compile-checked example for the rare PostgreSQL
operation sqlc cannot model. A real adapter belongs beside its owning feature,
accepts only the tracer-covered pgx methods it needs, and is constructed from
the use case's callback `pgx.Tx`. Do not grow a shared raw repository or expose
`Conn`/`PgConn`. The example's disposable-PostgreSQL test proves it commits and
rolls back with the caller-owned transaction. The complete review checklist is
in Nise's `docs/direct-pgx.md`.
